WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.138
<v Examiner>Exam case 4, continued.

2
00:00:03.138 --> 00:00:05.015
<v Examiner>Her two acid-base tables.

3
00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:11.060
These two tables are starred, six marks each, and her 2024 deck fills in every cell.

4
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:16.921
The trick is the order of each table's columns, because they run in opposite directions.

5
00:00:17.371 --> 00:00:17.989
<v Examiner>Six marks.

6
00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:21.389
<v Examiner>Complete the table for a metabolic acid-base imbalance.

7
00:00:21.389 --> 00:00:22.934
<v Examiner>How has blood pH changed?

8
00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:25.221
<v Examiner>How have hydrogen ion levels changed?

9
00:00:25.221 --> 00:00:27.694
<v Examiner>How does the bicarbonate buffer respond?

10
00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:31.094
<v Examiner>What ultimately happens to carbon dioxide in the blood?

11
00:00:31.094 --> 00:00:34.246
<v Examiner>And how does the breathing rate change as a result?

12
00:00:34.696 --> 00:00:39.624
In the metabolic table, the hydrogen ions change first, and breathing responds.

13
00:00:40.074 --> 00:00:40.710
Acidosis.

14
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.586
The pH has fallen, because hydrogen ions in the blood have increased.

15
00:00:45.586 --> 00:00:51.663
The buffer gets rid of hydrogen ions by converting them into carbon dioxide and water,

16
00:00:51.663 --> 00:00:54.560
so carbon dioxide in the blood increases.

17
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.082
The breathing rate increases, to get rid of that carbon dioxide.

18
00:00:59.532 --> 00:01:00.262
Alkalosis.

19
00:01:00.262 --> 00:01:04.279
The pH has risen, because hydrogen ions have decreased.

20
00:01:04.279 --> 00:01:10.341
The buffer replaces the lost hydrogen ions by turning carbon dioxide and water into

21
00:01:10.341 --> 00:01:15.600
hydrogen ions and bicarbonate, so carbon dioxide in the blood decreases.

22
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.055
The breathing rate decreases, to keep that carbon dioxide in.

23
00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:22.125
<v Examiner>Six marks.

24
00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:25.660
<v Examiner>Complete the table for a respiratory acid-base imbalance.

25
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.328
<v Examiner>How has ventilation changed, hyper or hypo?

26
00:01:28.328 --> 00:01:31.367
<v Examiner>How has that changed carbon dioxide in the blood?

27
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:33.848
<v Examiner>How does the bicarbonate buffer respond?

28
00:01:33.848 --> 00:01:36.391
<v Examiner>What ultimately happens to hydrogen ions?

29
00:01:36.391 --> 00:01:38.934
<v Examiner>And how does blood pH change as a result?

30
00:01:39.384 --> 00:01:43.672
In the respiratory table, breathing changes first, and the pH follows.

31
00:01:44.122 --> 00:01:46.798
Hyperventilation, which is Manaia.

32
00:01:46.798 --> 00:01:49.789
Carbon dioxide in the blood decreases.

33
00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:56.164
The buffer tries to replace the lost carbon dioxide by using up hydrogen ions and

34
00:01:56.164 --> 00:01:59.233
bicarbonate, so hydrogen ions decrease.

35
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:01.594
Blood pH increases: alkalosis.

36
00:02:02.044 --> 00:02:04.870
Hypoventilation, as after head trauma.

37
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.697
Carbon dioxide in the blood increases.

38
00:02:07.697 --> 00:02:14.316
The buffer gets rid of the extra carbon dioxide by combining it with water and converting

39
00:02:14.316 --> 00:02:19.151
it into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate, so hydrogen ions increase.

40
00:02:19.151 --> 00:02:21.308
Blood pH decreases: acidosis.

41
00:02:22.758 --> 00:02:23.350
<v Examiner>Two marks.

42
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.303
<v Examiner>Compare the tables you completed.

43
00:02:25.303 --> 00:02:26.427
<v Examiner>What do you notice?

44
00:02:26.877 --> 00:02:29.642
Her answer, from the line under her table.

45
00:02:29.642 --> 00:02:33.724
They're very similar processes: the bicarbonate buffer system.

46
00:02:33.724 --> 00:02:38.530
And carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions are linked: whatever happens to one,

47
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:39.912
happens to the other.

48
00:02:40.362 --> 00:02:41.050
<v Examiner>Once more.

49
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:41.877
<v Examiner>Both tables.

50
00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:46.966
Metabolic acidosis: hydrogen ions up;

51
00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:52.673
the buffer converts them into carbon dioxide and water, so carbon dioxide rises,

52
00:02:52.673 --> 00:02:54.385
and breathing speeds up.

53
00:02:54.385 --> 00:02:57.238
Metabolic alkalosis: hydrogen ions down;

54
00:02:57.238 --> 00:03:02.731
the buffer makes them from carbon dioxide and water, so carbon dioxide falls,

55
00:03:02.731 --> 00:03:04.158
and breathing slows.

56
00:03:04.158 --> 00:03:09.294
Hyperventilation: carbon dioxide down; the buffer uses up hydrogen ions,

57
00:03:09.294 --> 00:03:11.934
so they fall and pH rises: alkalosis.

58
00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:18.354
Hypoventilation: carbon dioxide up; the buffer makes hydrogen ions, so pH falls: acidosis.

59
00:03:18.354 --> 00:03:20.851
Compare them: it's the same buffer,

60
00:03:20.851 --> 00:03:24.988
and carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions always move together.
